  "summary": "Discover how Arabic builds words from a root of three letters and a pattern: دَرَسَ (studied), دَرَّسَ (taught), سَاعَدَ (helped), اِجْتَمَعَ (met), اِسْتَعْمَلَ (used).",
  "intro": "Back at the language school, Mr Karim explains one of the best tricks in Arabic. Most words come from a root of three letters, like د ر س (study), and a pattern adds the meaning: دَرَسَ (he studied), دَرَّسَ (he taught), مُدَرِّس (teacher), مَدْرَسَة (school). Verbs have about ten patterns, called Forms I to X.",
  "objectives": [
    "Find the three root letters in a word",
    "Tell Form I دَرَسَ from Form II دَرَّسَ",
    "Recognise the patterns فَاعَلَ, تَفَعَّلَ, اِفْتَعَلَ and اِسْتَفْعَلَ",
    "Guess the meaning of a new word from its root and pattern"
  ],
